    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can u give me a quick breakdown of the main differences between ETFs and mutual funds? mainly looking at how they handle liquidity and whether they're actively or passively managed. i need this for a client memo.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs) generally offer better liquidity due to their trading directly on an exchange like stocks. Based on demand, they can be bought and sold throughout the trading day at market prices. In contrast, Mutual Funds typically have bid-ask spreads and are priced at the end of the trading day, which may hinder immediate liquidity. Regarding management, ETFs are mostly passively managed, aiming to replicate the performance of a specific index, minimizing active decision-making. Mutual Funds, however, can be either actively or passively managed, with active funds having fund managers making investment decisions to outperform the market and passive funds aiming to mirror their chosen index or benchmark.",
